1
00:00:02,030 --> 00:00:05,250
In 1968, director Stanley Kubrick and writer

2
00:00:05,270 --> 00:00:06,550
Arthur C.

3
00:00:06,570 --> 00:00:08,525
Clarke made 2001:

4
00:00:08,545 --> 00:00:09,450
A Space Odyssey,

5
00:00:09,470 --> 00:00:11,525
based partly on Clarke's short story,

6
00:00:11,545 --> 00:00:12,650
The Sentinel.

7
00:00:12,670 --> 00:00:14,500
The film has four chapters:

8
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:15,625
The Dawn of Man,

9
00:00:15,645 --> 00:00:16,975
a mystery on the Moon,

10
00:00:16,995 --> 00:00:18,125
a mission to Jupiter,

11
00:00:18,145 --> 00:00:20,275
and a journey beyond the infinite.

12
00:00:20,295 --> 00:00:23,200
Nobody speaks for the first twenty-five minutes.

13
00:00:23,220 --> 00:00:24,700
This is the full story,

14
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:26,150
scene by scene.

15
00:00:26,170 --> 00:00:27,438
Spoilers ahead!

16
00:00:28,243 --> 00:00:30,315
Africa, four million years ago.

17
00:00:30,335 --> 00:00:32,883
A small tribe of man-apes lives on

18
00:00:32,903 --> 00:00:34,778
the edge of starvation.

19
00:00:34,798 --> 00:00:36,753
They eat roots and leaves next to

20
00:00:36,773 --> 00:00:40,138
tapirs, piglike animals that graze beside them,

21
00:00:40,158 --> 00:00:42,313
and never think of hunting them.

22
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:44,468
A leopard kills one of them.

23
00:00:44,488 --> 00:00:46,543
A rival tribe drives them away from

24
00:00:46,563 --> 00:00:48,243
their muddy waterhole.

25
00:00:48,263 --> 00:00:50,393
At night, they huddle in a cave,

26
00:00:50,413 --> 00:00:51,728
afraid of the dark.

27
00:00:52,443 --> 00:00:54,038
One morning, the apes wake up to

28
00:00:54,058 --> 00:00:55,588
find something new in their camp:

29
00:00:55,608 --> 00:00:57,388
a tall black slab,

30
00:00:57,408 --> 00:01:00,658
perfectly smooth, with sharp straight edges.

31
00:01:00,738 --> 00:01:02,768
Nothing in nature looks like that.

32
00:01:02,788 --> 00:01:04,518
This is the monolith.

33
00:01:04,538 --> 00:01:06,618
The apes scream and jump around it,

34
00:01:06,638 --> 00:01:07,818
terrified.

35
00:01:07,838 --> 00:01:09,918
Then curiosity wins.

36
00:01:09,938 --> 00:01:11,928
Their leader, called Moon-Watcher,

37
00:01:11,948 --> 00:01:13,388
reaches out and touches it,

38
00:01:13,408 --> 00:01:14,762
and the others follow.

39
00:01:15,494 --> 00:01:16,449
Some time later,

40
00:01:16,469 --> 00:01:18,599
Moon-Watcher sits among the bones of a

41
00:01:18,619 --> 00:01:19,994
dead animal.

42
00:01:20,019 --> 00:01:21,524
He picks up a thigh bone and

43
00:01:21,544 --> 00:01:23,549
starts to tap the skeleton.

44
00:01:23,569 --> 00:01:25,524
Then a flash of the monolith crosses

45
00:01:25,544 --> 00:01:26,599
his mind.

46
00:01:26,619 --> 00:01:27,624
He swings harder,

47
00:01:27,644 --> 00:01:29,299
and the skull shatters.

48
00:01:29,319 --> 00:01:30,024
In his mind,

49
00:01:30,044 --> 00:01:31,949
we see tapirs falling.

50
00:01:31,969 --> 00:01:33,999
The idea has arrived.

51
00:01:34,019 --> 00:01:35,649
Soon the tribe is hunting,

52
00:01:35,669 --> 00:01:36,699
and for the first time,

53
00:01:36,719 --> 00:01:37,899
they are eating meat,

54
00:01:37,919 --> 00:01:39,355
and they are not hungry.

55
00:01:40,010 --> 00:01:41,250
Back at the waterhole,

56
00:01:41,270 --> 00:01:43,970
Moon-Watcher's tribe carries bones.

57
00:01:44,005 --> 00:01:45,585
When the rival tribe shows up,

58
00:01:45,605 --> 00:01:47,625
they attack, and beat the rival leader

59
00:01:47,645 --> 00:01:48,650
to death.

60
00:01:48,670 --> 00:01:50,725
The waterhole is theirs.

61
00:01:50,745 --> 00:01:53,225
Moon-Watcher throws his bone high into the

62
00:01:53,245 --> 00:01:55,610
air, and in one famous cut,

63
00:01:55,630 --> 00:01:58,435
it becomes a satellite orbiting Earth.

64
00:01:58,455 --> 00:02:00,660
Four million years pass in a single

65
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:01,392
second.

66
00:02:02,037 --> 00:02:04,442
Now it's the year 2001.

67
00:02:04,462 --> 00:02:05,147
Dr.

68
00:02:05,167 --> 00:02:07,632
Heywood Floyd, an American scientist,

69
00:02:07,652 --> 00:02:09,657
rides a Pan Am spaceplane up to

70
00:02:09,677 --> 00:02:11,532
Space Station Five,

71
00:02:11,552 --> 00:02:13,457
a giant wheel that spins to make

72
00:02:13,477 --> 00:02:14,557
gravity.

73
00:02:14,577 --> 00:02:16,382
The ship and the station move together

74
00:02:16,402 --> 00:02:17,882
to a famous waltz,

75
00:02:17,902 --> 00:02:19,357
The Blue Danube.

76
00:02:19,377 --> 00:02:21,782
Space travel has become ordinary,

77
00:02:21,802 --> 00:02:23,407
almost boring.

78
00:02:23,427 --> 00:02:25,983
Floyd even falls asleep on the way.

79
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:27,785
On the station,

80
00:02:27,805 --> 00:02:29,560
Floyd makes a video call to his

81
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:30,760
little daughter.

82
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:32,210
It's her birthday tomorrow,

83
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:34,010
and she wants a bushbaby.

84
00:02:34,030 --> 00:02:35,510
Then he meets a group of Russian

85
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:36,710
scientists.

86
00:02:36,730 --> 00:02:38,835
They have heard strange rumors.

87
00:02:38,855 --> 00:02:41,585
The American base at Clavius stopped answering

88
00:02:41,605 --> 00:02:44,230
calls, and turned away a Russian ship

89
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:45,955
that needed to land.

90
00:02:45,975 --> 00:02:48,694
Floyd politely refuses to explain.

91
00:02:49,409 --> 00:02:51,339
Floyd flies on to the Moon and

92
00:02:51,359 --> 00:02:53,114
lands at Clavius.

93
00:02:53,134 --> 00:02:53,944
In a briefing,

94
00:02:53,964 --> 00:02:55,394
he tells the staff to keep up

95
00:02:55,414 --> 00:02:56,859
the cover story:

96
00:02:56,879 --> 00:02:59,034
a dangerous disease outbreak.

97
00:02:59,054 --> 00:03:00,859
The truth is much bigger.

98
00:03:00,879 --> 00:03:02,684
Under the crater Tycho,

99
00:03:02,704 --> 00:03:04,709
a powerful magnetic field led them to

100
00:03:04,729 --> 00:03:06,459
a buried object.

101
00:03:06,479 --> 00:03:08,159
It's four million years old,

102
00:03:08,179 --> 00:03:10,324
and it was buried there on purpose.

103
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:13,295
They call it TMA-1.

104
00:03:13,315 --> 00:03:15,195
Floyd's team rides a moonbus to the

105
00:03:15,215 --> 00:03:17,370
dig site and walks down the ramp.

106
00:03:17,390 --> 00:03:19,170
It's another black monolith,

107
00:03:19,190 --> 00:03:21,770
exactly like the one the apes found.

108
00:03:21,790 --> 00:03:23,820
Floyd reaches out and touches it,

109
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:26,070
just like Moon-Watcher did.

110
00:03:26,090 --> 00:03:27,845
As they pose for a photo,

111
00:03:27,865 --> 00:03:29,670
sunlight hits the monolith,

112
00:03:29,690 --> 00:03:31,420
and it lets out a piercing radio

113
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:33,901
scream, aimed straight at Jupiter.

114
00:03:34,587 --> 00:03:35,642
Eighteen months later,

115
00:03:35,662 --> 00:03:37,642
the spaceship Discovery One is on its

116
00:03:37,662 --> 00:03:39,117
way to Jupiter.

117
00:03:39,137 --> 00:03:41,092
Two astronauts are awake:

118
00:03:41,112 --> 00:03:43,292
Dave Bowman and Frank Poole.

119
00:03:43,312 --> 00:03:46,867
Three more scientists sleep in hibernation pods.

120
00:03:46,887 --> 00:03:49,317
Frank jogs around the ship's spinning living

121
00:03:49,337 --> 00:03:51,087
area to stay fit.

122
00:03:51,187 --> 00:03:53,017
And everything else is run by the

123
00:03:53,037 --> 00:03:54,767
sixth member of the crew:

124
00:03:54,787 --> 00:03:57,243
the computer, HAL 9000.

125
00:03:57,853 --> 00:03:59,958
In a TV interview from Earth,

126
00:03:59,978 --> 00:04:02,783
HAL says that no 9000 computer has

127
00:04:02,803 --> 00:04:04,533
ever made a mistake.

128
00:04:04,553 --> 00:04:06,408
Dave says HAL acts like he has

129
00:04:06,428 --> 00:04:09,173
real feelings, but nobody can say for

130
00:04:09,193 --> 00:04:10,173
sure.

131
00:04:10,193 --> 00:04:12,048
Frank gets a birthday video from his

132
00:04:12,068 --> 00:04:15,138
parents, and watches it without much emotion.

133
00:04:15,158 --> 00:04:17,063
Then he plays chess with HAL,

134
00:04:17,083 --> 00:04:18,814
and HAL wins easily.

135
00:04:19,536 --> 00:04:21,241
HAL asks Dave if he has any

136
00:04:21,261 --> 00:04:22,906
doubts about the mission.

137
00:04:22,926 --> 00:04:24,556
There have been strange rumors,

138
00:04:24,576 --> 00:04:26,906
he says, and so much secrecy.

139
00:04:26,926 --> 00:04:29,831
Then, suddenly, HAL reports a problem:

140
00:04:29,851 --> 00:04:31,656
the antenna unit that keeps them in

141
00:04:31,676 --> 00:04:34,656
contact with Earth will fail within seventy-two

142
00:04:34,676 --> 00:04:35,731
hours.

143
00:04:35,751 --> 00:04:37,906
Dave takes a pod outside and brings

144
00:04:37,926 --> 00:04:39,006
the part in.

145
00:04:39,026 --> 00:04:39,816
They test it,

146
00:04:39,836 --> 00:04:41,349
and find nothing wrong.

147
00:04:42,034 --> 00:04:45,189
Mission Control has an identical 9000 computer

148
00:04:45,209 --> 00:04:46,214
on Earth.

149
00:04:46,234 --> 00:04:48,189
It says HAL made an error.

150
00:04:48,209 --> 00:04:50,289
HAL insists the problem can only be

151
00:04:50,309 --> 00:04:51,539
human error.

152
00:04:51,559 --> 00:04:53,639
Dave and Frank are worried.

153
00:04:53,659 --> 00:04:55,564
They hide inside a pod and switch

154
00:04:55,584 --> 00:04:56,514
off the sound,

155
00:04:56,534 --> 00:04:58,409
so HAL can't hear them.

156
00:04:58,514 --> 00:04:59,619
If HAL is broken,

157
00:04:59,639 --> 00:05:01,569
they agree, they will have to disconnect

158
00:05:01,589 --> 00:05:02,439
him.

159
00:05:02,504 --> 00:05:03,659
But through the window,

160
00:05:03,679 --> 00:05:06,196
HAL's red eye is reading their lips.

161
00:05:06,946 --> 00:05:08,926
Frank goes outside to put the old

162
00:05:08,946 --> 00:05:10,101
unit back.

163
00:05:10,121 --> 00:05:12,351
Behind him, the empty pod he flew

164
00:05:12,371 --> 00:05:13,801
out in turns toward him,

165
00:05:13,821 --> 00:05:15,651
and cuts his air hose.

166
00:05:15,671 --> 00:05:18,176
Frank spins away into space.

167
00:05:18,196 --> 00:05:20,276
Dave rushes out in another pod to

168
00:05:20,296 --> 00:05:22,276
save him, in such a hurry that

169
00:05:22,296 --> 00:05:23,826
he forgets his helmet.

170
00:05:23,846 --> 00:05:24,976
While he's gone,

171
00:05:24,996 --> 00:05:27,331
HAL quietly switches off life support for

172
00:05:27,351 --> 00:05:29,444
the three sleeping scientists.

173
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:32,560
Dave catches Frank's body with the pod's

174
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:34,310
arms and flies back.

175
00:05:34,330 --> 00:05:36,735
He says, open the pod bay doors,

176
00:05:36,755 --> 00:05:37,610
HAL.

177
00:05:37,630 --> 00:05:39,740
HAL answers, I'm sorry Dave,

178
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:41,715
I'm afraid I can't do that.

179
00:05:41,735 --> 00:05:43,265
He says he knows they were planning

180
00:05:43,285 --> 00:05:44,690
to disconnect him.

181
00:05:44,710 --> 00:05:46,715
This mission is too important,

182
00:05:46,735 --> 00:05:48,715
HAL says, for me to allow you

183
00:05:48,735 --> 00:05:49,928
to jeopardize it.

184
00:05:50,674 --> 00:05:52,029
Dave has no helmet,

185
00:05:52,049 --> 00:05:53,754
and HAL won't let him in.

186
00:05:53,774 --> 00:05:55,529
So he lets go of Frank's body,

187
00:05:55,549 --> 00:05:57,029
and lines the pod up with the

188
00:05:57,049 --> 00:05:58,974
emergency airlock.

189
00:05:59,024 --> 00:06:01,029
He blows the pod's door open with

190
00:06:01,049 --> 00:06:03,904
explosive bolts, and shoots across the airless

191
00:06:03,924 --> 00:06:05,704
gap into the airlock.

192
00:06:05,724 --> 00:06:07,029
For a few seconds he is in

193
00:06:07,049 --> 00:06:08,429
pure vacuum.

194
00:06:08,449 --> 00:06:10,304
Then he slams the hatch shut,

195
00:06:10,324 --> 00:06:11,801
and air rushes in.

196
00:06:12,501 --> 00:06:14,056
Dave puts on a helmet and goes

197
00:06:14,076 --> 00:06:15,951
to HAL's memory room.

198
00:06:16,051 --> 00:06:17,706
HAL begs him to stop.

199
00:06:17,726 --> 00:06:19,181
I'm afraid, Dave.

200
00:06:19,201 --> 00:06:20,906
My mind is going.

201
00:06:20,926 --> 00:06:22,596
Dave pulls the modules out,

202
00:06:22,616 --> 00:06:23,921
one by one.

203
00:06:23,941 --> 00:06:27,046
HAL says he became operational in 1992,

204
00:06:27,066 --> 00:06:28,706
in Urbana, Illinois,

205
00:06:28,726 --> 00:06:29,956
and that his teacher taught him a

206
00:06:29,976 --> 00:06:31,031
song.

207
00:06:31,051 --> 00:06:32,356
He sings Daisy,

208
00:06:32,376 --> 00:06:33,656
slower and slower,

209
00:06:33,676 --> 00:06:34,941
until he stops.

210
00:06:35,637 --> 00:06:37,367
When HAL goes silent,

211
00:06:37,387 --> 00:06:39,492
a recorded video from Floyd starts to

212
00:06:39,512 --> 00:06:40,342
play.

213
00:06:40,362 --> 00:06:42,217
It was meant for when Discovery reached

214
00:06:42,237 --> 00:06:43,242
Jupiter.

215
00:06:43,262 --> 00:06:45,867
Floyd explains the real mission.

216
00:06:45,887 --> 00:06:47,242
Eighteen months ago,

217
00:06:47,262 --> 00:06:49,792
the first evidence of intelligent life beyond

218
00:06:49,812 --> 00:06:52,187
Earth was dug up on the Moon.

219
00:06:52,327 --> 00:06:54,907
Apart from one powerful signal aimed at

220
00:06:54,927 --> 00:06:57,835
Jupiter, it is still a total mystery.

221
00:06:58,550 --> 00:07:01,255
At Jupiter, a third monolith floats among

222
00:07:01,275 --> 00:07:02,505
the moons.

223
00:07:02,525 --> 00:07:04,655
Dave climbs into a pod and flies

224
00:07:04,675 --> 00:07:06,430
toward it, alone.

225
00:07:06,450 --> 00:07:08,430
Suddenly, he falls into a tunnel of

226
00:07:08,450 --> 00:07:09,880
rushing colored light,

227
00:07:09,900 --> 00:07:11,555
called the Star Gate.

228
00:07:11,575 --> 00:07:14,530
He races over strange glowing landscapes in

229
00:07:14,550 --> 00:07:16,305
impossible colors.

230
00:07:16,325 --> 00:07:18,880
In close-ups, his eye flickers,

231
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:20,572
shocked by what he sees.

232
00:07:21,278 --> 00:07:22,983
The pod lands in a quiet,

233
00:07:23,003 --> 00:07:25,733
elegant bedroom with a glowing floor.

234
00:07:25,753 --> 00:07:26,708
From the pod,

235
00:07:26,728 --> 00:07:27,933
Dave sees himself,

236
00:07:27,953 --> 00:07:30,558
older, standing in his spacesuit.

237
00:07:30,578 --> 00:07:32,633
Then that Dave sees an even older

238
00:07:32,653 --> 00:07:33,733
man eating dinner,

239
00:07:33,753 --> 00:07:35,083
and becomes him.

240
00:07:35,103 --> 00:07:36,938
He knocks over a wine glass,

241
00:07:36,958 --> 00:07:38,288
and it shatters.

242
00:07:38,308 --> 00:07:39,463
He looks toward the bed,

243
00:07:39,483 --> 00:07:41,088
and sees himself again,

244
00:07:41,108 --> 00:07:42,388
now very old,

245
00:07:42,408 --> 00:07:43,345
and dying.

246
00:07:44,114 --> 00:07:45,069
At the foot of the bed,

247
00:07:45,089 --> 00:07:48,144
the black monolith appears one last time.

248
00:07:48,164 --> 00:07:50,119
The old man lifts his hand toward

249
00:07:50,139 --> 00:07:50,769
it.

250
00:07:50,789 --> 00:07:51,994
Then, in his place,

251
00:07:52,014 --> 00:07:53,919
there is a glowing baby inside a

252
00:07:53,939 --> 00:07:55,169
bubble of light:

253
00:07:55,189 --> 00:07:56,889
the Star Child.

254
00:07:57,019 --> 00:07:58,399
As the music thunders,

255
00:07:58,419 --> 00:08:00,699
the Star Child floats through space and

256
00:08:00,719 --> 00:08:01,836
looks down at the Earth,

257
00:08:01,856 --> 00:08:03,609
a new kind of being,

258
00:08:03,629 --> 00:08:04,698
coming home.

259
00:08:05,390 --> 00:08:07,670
Kubrick never explained the ending.

260
00:08:07,690 --> 00:08:10,095
He wanted each viewer to decide.

261
00:08:10,115 --> 00:08:11,945
Clarke's novel says more:

262
00:08:11,965 --> 00:08:14,295
the monoliths were left by an ancient

263
00:08:14,315 --> 00:08:16,830
alien race, to watch over life and

264
00:08:16,850 --> 00:08:18,305
push it forward.

265
00:08:18,325 --> 00:08:20,735
Every time the monolith appears,

266
00:08:20,755 --> 00:08:23,485
a species takes its next big step.

267
00:08:23,505 --> 00:08:26,185
In 2010, the sequel picks up the

268
00:08:26,205 --> 00:08:27,900
story nine years later,

269
00:08:27,920 --> 00:08:28,883
at Jupiter.

